<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  launchd USER AGENT for the ctxroute HTTP injection gate.
  NOT INSTALLED. Copy to ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.ctxroute.http.plist,
  edit the two paths below, then: launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) <path>
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  ⚠️ AGENT, NEVER A DAEMON — and Apple states the difference in exactly the terms
     that decide it for us. TN2083: "An agent is a process that runs in the
     background on behalf of a particular user. Agents are useful because they
     can do things that daemons can't, like reliably access the user's home
     directory". This service reads the user's documents: it is an agent.
     "A third party launchd agent should be installed by adding a property list
     file to the ~/Library/LaunchAgents directory (to be invoked just for this
     user)". Login is the trigger: "When a user logs in, a per-user launchd is
     started" (Creating Launch Daemons and Agents).

  🔴 SOURCE STATUS, STATED AND NOT HIDDEN. Apple's web copies of this material
     are ARCHIVED: "Creating Launch Daemons and Agents" last updated 2016-09-13,
     and TN2083 carries "This document is no longer being updated", its examples
     dating from Mac OS X 10.5. Apple's launchd.plist(5) is not published at a
     current URL; the AUTHORITY for the installed system is `man launchd.plist`
     ON THE TARGET MAC. Every key below is one Apple documented; none of their
     DEFAULT values is restated here, precisely because those are what an archived
     page is least trustworthy about. Read the man page before you install.
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>

    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.ctxroute.http</string>

    <!--
      ⚠️ ABSOLUTE PATHS, MANDATORY. Edit BOTH entries: the node binary (on Apple
         silicon Homebrew installs it under /opt/homebrew/bin, on Intel under
         /usr/local/bin — check, do not assume) and the repository location.
      🛑 NEVER wrap this in `sh -c`. A shell would change the process launchd
         supervises and this repository forbids shell indirection on every layer.
    -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/local/bin/node</string>
        <string>/Users/CHANGE_ME/ctxroute/src/hooks/http-server.js</string>
    </array>

    <!--
      ⚠️ THE PORT TRAVELS BY ENVIRONMENT — same number as the harness hook URL on
         the other side. Two ends, one value.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>CTXROUTE_HTTP_PORT</key>
        <string>8787</string>
    </dict>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🔴 SOCKET ACTIVATION: THE CAPABILITY EXISTS HERE AND WE CANNOT REACH IT
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      On Linux the socket unit removes the whole restart burst: systemd owns the
      listening socket, so a stale-code exit costs nothing and connections wait
      in the backlog. launchd has the SAME capability — the `Sockets` key, whose
      shape Apple documents ("Creating Launch Daemons and Agents", updated
      2016-09-13): a `Listeners` dictionary with `SockServiceName` / `SockType` /
      `SockFamily`.
      🛑 WHAT WE CANNOT DO IS PICK THE SOCKET UP. Apple's own instruction is
         "check in with launchd as part of your daemon initialization", and the
         modern check-in is `launch_activate_socket` — a C function of the XPC
         framework. There is NO environment protocol here: nothing equivalent to
         systemd's $LISTEN_FDS/$LISTEN_PID, so nothing a JavaScript process can
         read.
      📐 MEASURED 2026-08-20, Node 22.15.1: the 54 builtin modules were scanned
         for any export matching /launch|activate_socket|listen_fds/ — ZERO. The
         `net` documentation never mentions launchd. A native addon would close
         the gap and is REFUSED: this framework must install from a plain clone.
      ⇒ THE `Sockets` KEY IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. Declaring it would make launchd
         hold a socket that this daemon can never accept from — the port would
         still be bound by the process, and the plist would describe something
         that is not happening. A capability we do not have is DECLARED, never
         simulated.
      ⇒ macOS therefore keeps the EAGER model below (RunAtLoad + KeepAlive), the
         one Linux just left. That asymmetry is normal: a thin unit per OS,
         never a lowest common denominator.
    -->

    <!-- ⚠️ Start at load, i.e. at login for an agent in ~/Library/LaunchAgents. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      RESTART ON FAILURE — and why the key reads `false`
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🛑 `SuccessfulExit` is NOT "should it succeed": it selects WHICH exit gets
         a respawn. TN2083 documents the positive form —
         `KeepAlive = { SuccessfulExit = true }` = "Run on demand as long as you
         exit successfully", i.e. restart only after a ZERO exit. We need the
         MIRROR of that: restart only when the process exits NON-ZERO.
      ⇒ exit 90 (stale code detected) brings the agent back; a deliberate exit 0
         would not. That asymmetry is the whole reason `http-server.js` refuses to
         stand down with a zero status — read EXIT_STALE_CODE there.
      ⚠️ 90 IS NOT AN ARBITRARY NUMBER, and launchd is not where it was decided:
         the first choice, 75, collided with systemd's `TEMPFAIL` alias and with
         an example in systemd's own manual (see the .service file). launchd has
         no such alias table, so this key would have worked either way — the
         number is shared across the three OSes on purpose, and it is DERIVED
         from the code by `test/service-units-gate.test.js`, never re-typed.
      🛑 NEVER replace this dictionary with a bare <true/>. Plain KeepAlive
         restarts on EVERY exit, including one the operator asked for, and the
         only way left to stop the agent would be to unload it — a supervisor
         that fights the operator gets uninstalled.
    -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <!--
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      CLEAN STOP
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      ⚠️ Nothing to declare, and that is the point. launchd signals the job to
         quit: "When the user logs out, it sends a SIGTERM signal to all of the
         user agents that it started" (Creating Launch Daemons and Agents), and
         SIGKILL follows if it does not go. The process dies on node's default
         SIGTERM handling; we install no handler because there is nothing to
         flush. Stop by hand = `launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.ctxroute.http`.
      ⚠️ ExitTimeOut and ThrottleInterval are DELIBERATELY UNSET: launchd's own
         defaults apply and this file will not restate numbers it could not read
         at a current official source. Consequence to expect, not a defect: after
         a code change the agent comes back after launchd's respawn throttle,
         not instantly.

      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      THE LOG IS BOUNDED BY macOS — NEVER BY US
      ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      🛑 StandardOutPath / StandardErrorPath ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and adding
         them is the single most likely mistake against this file. A plist log
         path is a plain file that launchd NEVER rotates and NEVER caps: on a
         machine running for years that is a monotonic writer, i.e. a dated
         outage, and this project treats an uncapped writer as an architecture
         bug rather than an operations chore.
      ✅ Leaving them out is not "losing the logs": TN2083, "If you don't supply
         these properties […] In Mac OS X 10.5 and later, launchd will capture any
         output to these streams and redirect it to ASL" — the system log, which
         macOS caps and rolls over on its own. Read it with `log show --predicate
         'process == "node"'`. The OS already owns this problem; we do not
         reimplement it.
    -->

</dict>
</plist>
